WEBVTT

00:00.110 --> 00:02.660
- So this is Sal Khan,
founder of the Khan Academy.

00:02.660 --> 00:05.680
And this is a very exciting
Skype call that we're on.

00:05.680 --> 00:07.260
I'm with Ben Hedrick,

00:07.260 --> 00:10.080
who's the lead for AP Calculus.

00:10.080 --> 00:11.980
What do you at the College Board?

00:12.830 --> 00:16.570
- Really, anything with AP
Calculus and AP Statistics

00:16.570 --> 00:18.900
is something that I have a
little bit of control over.

00:18.900 --> 00:20.800
So for example, for AP Calculus,

00:20.800 --> 00:23.640
anything with the curriculum,
anything with the assessment

00:23.640 --> 00:25.837
is something that I work with.

00:25.837 --> 00:29.020
- And I'm sure that you're as excited

00:29.020 --> 00:30.650
as I am and, you know,

00:30.650 --> 00:33.040
several hundred thousands of
students are around the country

00:33.040 --> 00:35.720
for the AP exams that are coming up

00:35.720 --> 00:38.600
in roughly a little less than a week.

00:38.600 --> 00:42.294
And I thought a fun
place to start would be

00:42.294 --> 00:44.460
when you guys write the test,

00:44.460 --> 00:46.020
what are you trying to assess?

00:46.020 --> 00:48.290
What's the spirit of the questions?

00:48.290 --> 00:51.622
Both on the multiple choice
and the free response section.

00:51.622 --> 00:53.620
- So what we're looking for from students

00:53.620 --> 00:57.520
is evidence of good, conceptual
understanding in calculus.

00:57.520 --> 00:59.890
Now, we have some things that
are gonna test procedures,

00:59.890 --> 01:02.740
but really, we want students
to understand what's going on

01:02.740 --> 01:05.560
with the calculus rather
than just calculating things

01:05.560 --> 01:07.110
or putting numbers on paper.

01:07.110 --> 01:09.780
A lot of thinking
questions, a lot of insight

01:09.780 --> 01:12.240
to make sure that whatever
good calculus those students

01:12.240 --> 01:13.510
have learned in their courses,

01:13.510 --> 01:15.920
we're assessing it on the AP exam.

01:15.920 --> 01:20.360
- And I've done a bunch of
example problems for the AP exam,

01:20.360 --> 01:22.724
and yeah, my sense of it is

01:22.724 --> 01:26.760
you sometimes will do
some of the minutiae work

01:26.760 --> 01:28.140
or you'll give the formula,

01:28.140 --> 01:29.380
and you're really trying to understand

01:29.380 --> 01:30.880
whether students understand the essence

01:30.880 --> 01:31.800
of what an integral is,

01:31.800 --> 01:34.680
or the essence of derivative
as a rate of change,

01:34.680 --> 01:36.240
or the slope of a tangent line.

01:36.240 --> 01:39.320
So, if you were preparing for this exam,

01:39.320 --> 01:40.420
if you were the students,

01:40.420 --> 01:42.280
what you suggest to them?

01:42.280 --> 01:44.170
You know, obviously we have
a little less than a week

01:44.170 --> 01:45.100
for this current test,

01:45.100 --> 01:46.960
so what advice would you
have for those students?

01:46.960 --> 01:49.040
And then just generally, as
students go through the year,

01:49.040 --> 01:51.189
how should they think about it?

01:51.189 --> 01:52.930
- Yeah, the advice I would give

01:52.930 --> 01:54.980
is dependent on how much
time the students have

01:54.980 --> 01:57.080
to prepare, so the advice that I give

01:57.080 --> 01:58.900
at the beginning of the year
is different from the advice

01:58.900 --> 02:01.662
that I give now, several
days before the exam.

02:01.662 --> 02:03.970
Getting ready for it, first, relax.

02:03.970 --> 02:05.660
You know, this is a calculus exam.

02:05.660 --> 02:07.990
We're not gonna be throwing
anything at you from left field.

02:07.990 --> 02:09.140
It's calculus.

02:09.140 --> 02:10.360
You need to know limits.

02:10.360 --> 02:11.560
You need to know derivatives.

02:11.560 --> 02:12.920
You need to know integrals.

02:12.920 --> 02:15.730
In terms of preparation, make
sure you're taking the time

02:15.730 --> 02:17.254
to review the basics.

02:17.254 --> 02:18.500
Every question that they see

02:18.500 --> 02:20.500
is gonna hit one of those big topics,

02:20.500 --> 02:22.570
so make sure you know
your derivative rules,

02:22.570 --> 02:24.546
make sure you know your integral rules.

02:24.546 --> 02:26.100
Take a look at the old exams

02:26.100 --> 02:28.100
and see how we're asking questions.

02:28.100 --> 02:30.940
And you'll at the patterns and
see that we're always asking

02:30.940 --> 02:33.420
about understanding
what a derivative means,

02:33.420 --> 02:36.920
understanding integration
or the idea of accumulation.

02:36.920 --> 02:38.730
There are no secrets on the exams,

02:38.730 --> 02:41.130
so really stick with what we've done.

02:41.130 --> 02:42.650
Make sure that you're thinking of ways

02:42.650 --> 02:44.220
to apply to your good knowledge,

02:44.220 --> 02:46.611
and just review your basics.

02:46.611 --> 02:49.060
- And my experience with the
exam is there's a lot of,

02:49.060 --> 02:52.020
as you just said, really the
mainstream, meaty topics.

02:52.020 --> 02:54.720
You know, chain rule, fundamental theorem,

02:54.720 --> 02:57.260
or theorems of calculus, things like that.

02:57.260 --> 02:58.950
What's your sense of the more,

02:58.950 --> 03:00.920
what I would concern maybe a
little bit more, the minutiae,

03:00.920 --> 03:02.770
some of the more special case derivatives,

03:02.770 --> 03:06.284
or special case integrals,
you know, arc tangent and,

03:06.284 --> 03:10.355
how much does that play into the exam?

03:10.355 --> 03:12.250
- You know, I think, Sal,
you've actually answered

03:12.250 --> 03:13.570
your own question in asking that,

03:13.570 --> 03:15.470
is it is the minutiae.

03:15.470 --> 03:18.020
So really, at this point in
time, if you're getting ready

03:18.020 --> 03:20.320
for the exam, spending time on minutiae

03:20.320 --> 03:22.836
is not where you're gonna be
best served with your time.

03:22.836 --> 03:24.250
You should be hitting the major things,

03:24.250 --> 03:26.040
and you said it right at
the beginning, chain rule.

03:26.040 --> 03:28.660
I mean, any kind of question
we do with derivatives,

03:28.660 --> 03:31.000
you know, if you're doing maxes or mins,

03:31.000 --> 03:32.440
if you're doing tangent lines,

03:32.440 --> 03:34.350
if you're doing a related rates problem,

03:34.350 --> 03:36.870
they're all derivatives,
derivatives, derivatives.

03:36.870 --> 03:39.090
And if you're having
trouble with chain rule,

03:39.090 --> 03:41.940
it doesn't matter how much
minutiae you spend time with,

03:41.940 --> 03:43.480
the chain rule's gonna come up.

03:43.480 --> 03:46.680
So I would say don't sweat the minutiae.

03:46.680 --> 03:48.210
There might be something on the exam

03:48.210 --> 03:50.430
that you haven't reviewed
as much as you want to,

03:50.430 --> 03:53.649
but it's gonna be one
single, solitary question.

03:53.649 --> 03:56.050
Basic derivatives are going to form, well,

03:56.050 --> 03:57.860
the basis for all those problems.

03:57.860 --> 03:59.580
So that's where your time is best spent.

03:59.580 --> 04:00.770
- Yeah, I definitely get that sense.

04:00.770 --> 04:03.210
Especially, you know, how
do you interpret the first,

04:03.210 --> 04:06.910
second, you know, derivatives, concavity.

04:07.830 --> 04:10.140
Things like that, those
seem to show up a lot.

04:10.140 --> 04:13.930
But once again, that's a
conceptual idea of derivatives.

04:13.930 --> 04:15.390
And on the integration side,

04:15.390 --> 04:17.280
I've been doing a bunch
of the free response.

04:17.280 --> 04:18.840
It just seems like, I mean, you know,

04:18.840 --> 04:21.300
what happens if you swap
the bounds of integration,

04:21.300 --> 04:22.620
or if you add integrals.

04:22.620 --> 04:25.300
So a lot of the properties and
the conceptual understanding

04:25.300 --> 04:26.734
of what an integral is,

04:26.734 --> 04:29.740
but not necessarily the fancy tricks.

04:29.740 --> 04:31.840
At least at this point,
if you're studying.

04:31.840 --> 04:34.080
- Absolutely, there's
nothing that we go after

04:34.080 --> 04:35.010
the fancy tricks.

04:35.010 --> 04:37.200
We're not trying to ask trick questions

04:37.200 --> 04:38.800
or play any gotcha moments on it.

04:38.800 --> 04:40.880
It's really assessment of calculus.

04:40.880 --> 04:42.330
And for the students who
have been going through

04:42.330 --> 04:43.960
these great courses all year,

04:43.960 --> 04:46.280
this is a wonderful opportunity to show us

04:46.280 --> 04:47.590
everything that you know

04:47.590 --> 04:49.731
and get a really great score on the exam.

04:49.731 --> 04:51.440
- And what about calculator?

04:51.440 --> 04:52.960
I mean, I was doing some
of the free response.

04:52.960 --> 04:54.440
That first part of the free response,

04:54.440 --> 04:56.940
knowing your calculator well helps.

04:56.940 --> 04:57.890
- Oh, absolutely.

04:57.890 --> 04:59.250
The calculator's a tool,

04:59.250 --> 05:00.930
and like any tool, you want to make sure

05:00.930 --> 05:03.390
that you're applying it
appropriately and strategically,

05:03.390 --> 05:05.780
which sometimes means
using your calculator,

05:05.780 --> 05:07.737
and using it well and correctly,

05:07.737 --> 05:09.500
but other times not using your calculator.

05:09.500 --> 05:11.250
I mean, even on the free response section

05:11.250 --> 05:13.220
where the calculator is being used,

05:13.220 --> 05:15.220
we expect students to show their work.

05:15.220 --> 05:16.800
If you're taking an integral,

05:16.800 --> 05:19.290
we wanna see that you've
actually taken that integral.

05:19.290 --> 05:21.470
Now, the calculator
will do the work of it,

05:21.470 --> 05:23.080
but we wanna see the notation that says

05:23.080 --> 05:24.680
this is the integral you calculated,

05:24.680 --> 05:26.930
and this is the answer you obtained.

05:26.930 --> 05:29.250
And then do whatever you
will with that answer

05:29.250 --> 05:30.560
as required by the question,

05:30.560 --> 05:33.180
but good communication through writing,

05:33.180 --> 05:35.942
even on the calculator
section, is necessary.

05:35.942 --> 05:39.485
- And for students taking the BC exam,

05:39.485 --> 05:42.240
above and beyond the core differentiation,

05:42.240 --> 05:44.530
integration, you know,
a lot of what you learn

05:44.530 --> 05:46.850
about parametric equations
is actually just an extension

05:46.850 --> 05:48.980
of what you learn in
differentiation, integration.

05:48.980 --> 05:52.420
But probably some of the convergence tests

05:52.420 --> 05:55.320
are something to become
pretty familiar with.

05:55.320 --> 05:57.166
- That would be correct, yes.

05:57.166 --> 06:01.060
- And in terms of grading of the exam,

06:01.060 --> 06:02.882
you know, some people,

06:02.882 --> 06:06.081
to get a five or a high,

06:06.081 --> 06:07.820
my understanding is you
don't need to answer

06:07.820 --> 06:10.493
absolutely every question perfectly.

06:10.493 --> 06:12.570
- Well, like any test,
if you want to do well,

06:12.570 --> 06:14.140
you don't have to have perfection on it,

06:14.140 --> 06:17.400
and this is a test where
students are coming in

06:17.400 --> 06:20.200
and it's a three hour timed test,

06:20.200 --> 06:22.340
well, a little over three hour timed test,

06:22.340 --> 06:25.450
and you know, you can do
a lot of really good work

06:25.450 --> 06:27.830
and earn a five pretty easily.

06:27.830 --> 06:30.380
So I wouldn't tell anyone
to go in there worrying

06:30.380 --> 06:33.570
about trying to figure out
what magical numbers they need

06:33.570 --> 06:36.100
to get in order to get a
perfect score on the test

06:36.100 --> 06:37.210
or to get a five on the test

06:37.210 --> 06:39.720
or whatever it is that they're going for.

06:39.720 --> 06:41.760
Just go in like you would on any test,

06:41.760 --> 06:43.300
do your best on every question,

06:43.300 --> 06:45.046
get every point that you can,

06:45.046 --> 06:47.110
and hopefully it'll work out fine for you.

06:47.110 --> 06:48.530
- What advice would you have for students,

06:48.530 --> 06:49.680
especially on the free response

06:49.680 --> 06:50.920
where there's multiple sections,

06:50.920 --> 06:52.330
and if, you know, on part A they say,

06:52.330 --> 06:53.300
what, that, that's,

06:53.300 --> 06:55.070
I don't get what that, what,

06:55.070 --> 06:57.010
you know, should they
skip to the next free,

06:57.010 --> 06:58.718
what should they do?

06:58.718 --> 07:02.660
- I actually have very
common advice on that one

07:02.660 --> 07:04.480
and that's to make sure
that students are trying

07:04.480 --> 07:06.500
every part of a free response question.

07:06.500 --> 07:08.500
One of the misconceptions students have

07:08.500 --> 07:09.570
is exactly what you said.

07:09.570 --> 07:11.920
If you hit part A and you're
freaking out a little bit

07:11.920 --> 07:14.195
because you don't know
what part A is about,

07:14.195 --> 07:15.950
that's okay, move on to part B.

07:15.950 --> 07:18.460
There are some questions
where the answer for part b

07:18.460 --> 07:21.340
depends on part A, but for
the majority of the questions,

07:21.340 --> 07:22.590
they're separate pieces.

07:22.590 --> 07:24.560
So part A, part B, part C,

07:24.560 --> 07:25.720
if there's a part D,

07:25.720 --> 07:28.140
they might all be asking
very different things.

07:28.140 --> 07:31.020
And if you have trouble even with part C,

07:31.020 --> 07:32.520
that doesn't mean the question

07:32.520 --> 07:34.400
gets progressively more difficult.

07:34.400 --> 07:35.920
Give part D a try.

07:35.920 --> 07:38.380
And a lot of the things
that we do give points for

07:38.380 --> 07:40.940
are good, solid calculus work.

07:40.940 --> 07:42.660
So if you can set up a problem,

07:42.660 --> 07:44.350
maybe you don't have time to finish it,

07:44.350 --> 07:46.520
maybe you make some
mistakes along the way,

07:46.520 --> 07:48.650
the answer point is only one little point

07:48.650 --> 07:49.720
out of the nine.

07:49.720 --> 07:51.320
There are other points for the setup

07:51.320 --> 07:53.470
and the conceptual
understanding of the problem,

07:53.470 --> 07:56.280
and a lot of students have
good conceptual understanding

07:56.280 --> 07:57.558
that they could set up,

07:57.558 --> 07:59.560
pick up a point here,
pick up a point there,

07:59.560 --> 08:01.340
but they panic a little bit,

08:01.340 --> 08:02.800
and they move on to another question

08:02.800 --> 08:04.214
and never come back.

08:04.214 --> 08:06.730
And that's okay if you want to
move on to another question,

08:06.730 --> 08:08.440
but absolutely I would say,

08:08.440 --> 08:10.338
if you're having trouble with part A,

08:10.338 --> 08:12.769
that's all right, take a look at part B.

08:12.769 --> 08:15.176
- And one thing that I've observed is

08:15.176 --> 08:16.770
a lot of these questions, at
first when you look at 'em,

08:16.770 --> 08:18.120
like oh wow, this looks like some really,

08:18.120 --> 08:19.660
you know, super deep thing,

08:19.660 --> 08:23.060
but it really is some
core basic calculus ideas,

08:23.060 --> 08:24.780
and that if you're on the right track,

08:24.780 --> 08:26.260
it's actually quite simple.

08:27.150 --> 08:29.450
Would you say it's fair
if a student finds himself

08:29.450 --> 08:31.160
doing a very hairy calculation

08:31.160 --> 08:34.349
that they might question
whether they need to?

08:34.349 --> 08:36.100
- I would go beyond might.

08:36.100 --> 08:37.660
They should question what they're doing.

08:37.660 --> 08:39.420
If you've done something
where you feel like

08:39.420 --> 08:41.090
you need to prove a new calculus theorem

08:41.090 --> 08:42.100
in order to answer it,

08:42.100 --> 08:44.915
something has gone
terribly, terribly wrong.

08:44.915 --> 08:46.250
And also, if you feel like you need

08:46.250 --> 08:47.840
to bring in some sort of mathematics

08:47.840 --> 08:49.660
you've never seen before in calculus,

08:50.980 --> 08:53.700
as much as I hate to say it,
you know, this is calculus.

08:53.700 --> 08:56.580
It's limits, it's
derivatives, it's integrals.

08:56.580 --> 08:58.460
Even though there's a lot
of wealth in that one,

08:58.460 --> 09:00.630
if you're doing something beyond that,

09:00.630 --> 09:02.859
probably something has gone wrong.

09:02.859 --> 09:04.040
- Yeah, yeah.

09:04.040 --> 09:04.770
Well, thanks.

09:04.770 --> 09:06.240
I think, you know,

09:06.240 --> 09:09.180
thousands of AP students are
gonna really appreciate that.

09:09.180 --> 09:12.749
Any other parting words
for the coming test?

09:12.749 --> 09:14.400
- You know, it's the same thing

09:14.400 --> 09:15.760
that I used to tell my students

09:15.760 --> 09:17.160
when I was teaching AP Calculus,

09:17.160 --> 09:18.520
this is just another test,

09:18.520 --> 09:21.090
and the beauty of AP is
the teachers have been

09:21.090 --> 09:23.570
prepping their students since
the beginning of the year.

09:23.570 --> 09:24.920
It's one of the few tests

09:24.920 --> 09:27.248
where everyone knows what's coming,

09:27.248 --> 09:30.210
so when you sit down and you've
got free response questions,

09:30.210 --> 09:32.030
you've got multiple choice questions,

09:32.030 --> 09:34.320
you know, there really
shouldn't be any surprises.

09:34.320 --> 09:36.693
This is the stuff they've seen all year.

09:36.693 --> 09:38.361
They know what to do.

09:38.361 --> 09:40.615
Take a moment, take a breath,

09:40.615 --> 09:42.350
take a look at the problem,

09:42.350 --> 09:43.580
get all the points that you can.

09:43.580 --> 09:44.940
Show us that good calculus.

09:44.940 --> 09:46.320
- Awesome, and I'll throw in a plug.

09:46.320 --> 09:48.720
We have tons of resources
for the students as well,

09:48.720 --> 09:51.520
and we're gonna have our
office hours on Monday.

09:51.520 --> 09:52.920
So, super exciting.

09:52.920 --> 09:54.230
Thanks a bunch, Ben.

09:54.230 --> 09:55.670
- No, thanks for having me.
